+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **3DGS vs 2DGS:** Using 3DGS instead of 2DGS causes depth inconsistencies across views, leading to TSDF producing incorrect fusions with warped/disconnected surfaces [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Prior Method Failure:** Prior SOTA generalizable GS method [MVSGaussian](15257-mvsgaussian.md) fails to give coherent reconstructions on TSDF fusion of rendered depth maps [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Feature Choice:** Using only image features or DINOv2 features yields poor results compared to MASt3R. MASt3R provides an 11.11% boost over image-only features, while DINOv2 provides only a 0.85% boost [S1, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is extremely fast, approximately 0.8s (reproduced 0.88s) on an RTX A6000 GPU for 3 views [S1, unofficial]. This is nearly 2 orders of magnitude faster than prior state-of-the-art feed-forward sparse view reconstruction methods based on implicit volume rendering (e.g., UfoRecon at 66s, GeoTransfer at 32s) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is nearly 2 orders of magnitude faster than prior state-of-the-art feed-forward sparse view reconstruction based on volume rendering of implicit representations [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Performance:**
-    *   On the DTU test set, SparSplat achieves PSNR 28.33, SSIM 0.938, and LPIPS 0.073 for novel-view synthesis [S1, unofficial].
-    *   For 3D reconstruction (Chamfer Distance), it achieves a mean CD of 1.04 on the DTU dataset [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Using MASt3R features provides an 11.11% boost in reconstruction quality over using only image features, compared to a 0.85% boost from DINOv2 [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Adding depth loss $L_{depth}$ improves mean Chamfer distance by 71.8% (from 4.37 to 1.04) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:**
+    *   Inference time: 0.8s (reproduced 0.88s) on RTX A6000 [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Comparison: 66s for UfoRecon, 32s for GeoTransfer [S1, unofficial].
+    *   DTU Test Set (NVS): PSNR 28.33, SSIM 0.938, LPIPS 0.073 [S1, unofficial].
+    *   DTU Test Set (Reconstruction): Mean Chamfer Distance 1.04 (with MASt3R and depth loss) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance improves with the addition of depth loss $L_{depth}$, improving mean chamfer distance by 71.8% (from 4.37 to 1.04) [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:**
-    *   Fast, feed-forward inference suitable for real-time applications.
-    *   Superior reconstruction quality and NVS performance compared to implicit methods (e.g., SparseNeuS) and prior generalizable GS methods (e.g., MVSGaussian) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Produces coherent 3D meshes via TSDF fusion, which prior 3DGS-based generalizable methods fail to do due to depth inconsistencies [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Strong generalization on BlendedMVS and Tanks and Temples datasets [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Fast multi-view reconstruction and novel view synthesis from sparse views (3 views) [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Coherent 3D geometry reconstruction due to 2DGS representation [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Strong generalization on unseen scenes (DTU, BlendedMVS, Tanks and Temples) [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:**
-    *   Requires known camera poses for input images [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Performance relies on the quality of foundation model features (MASt3R/DINOv2) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   TSDF fusion parameters (voxel size) need tuning; incorrect settings may lead to poor mesh quality [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When fast, generalizable 3D reconstruction and novel-view synthesis from sparse (3-view) inputs are required, and an explicit mesh is needed.
-*   **When not to use:** When camera poses are unknown, or when dense input views are available and per-scene optimization is acceptable for higher quality.
+    *   Requires camera poses for input images [S1, unofficial].
+    *   TSDF fusion quality depends on the consistency of the rendered depths, which is why 2DGS is preferred over 3DGS [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When fast, generalizable 3D reconstruction and NVS from sparse views (e.g., 3 images) is required, and explicit mesh extraction is needed.
+*   **When not to use:** When dense input views are available and per-scene optimization is acceptable, or when 3DGS is strictly required for other reasons (though it harms TSDF fusion here).
 
 ## Background
-SparSplat was proposed by Shubhendu Jena et al. in 2025 [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the MVSGaussian framework [48] and MVSFormer++ [6], extending them to use 2D Gaussian Splatting and foundation model features (DINOv2, MASt3R) for generalizable sparse-view reconstruction. It addresses the limitation of prior generalizable GS methods which were designed for novel-view synthesis and failed to produce coherent 3D meshes via TSDF fusion [S1, unofficial]. The method is evaluated on the DTU, BlendedMVS, and Tanks and Temples datasets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** SparSplat was proposed by Shubhendu Jena et al. in 2025 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **History:** It extends the MVSGaussian framework [48] by replacing 3D primitives with 2D Gaussian Splatting (2DGS) [28] to improve geometric consistency. It incorporates features from foundation models DINOv2 [59] and MASt3R [41], inspired by MVSFormer++ [6].
+*   **Usage:** Evaluated on DTU sparse 3D reconstruction benchmark, BlendedMVS, and Tanks and Temples datasets [S1, unofficial].
 
